# Joined native publication development qualification

The frozen eight-event sequence completed with six accepted events, one stale
branch rejected by the actual native publication CAS, and one unresolved clamp
task retained as `residual_pending`. This adds real accepted-state publication
and recovery to the constructed controller qualification. It does not complete
real-repository E06 or estimate system token savings.

The native trajectory published a full checkpoint at generation 0, then a
delta at generation 1. A real service process exited with code 94 after native
seal persistence and before CAS. A fresh process loaded the pending native
seal and receipts, checked the entire parent chain and exact local evidence,
re-executed visible and audit behavior, then completed native CAS and WAL using
the explicitly new development recovery adapter. It reached generation 2.
Another fresh event consumed that native stored accepted source, executed the
next checks, and published generation 3. A branch based on generation 0 reached
native publication and lost with `stale_parent` / `generation_mismatch`.

| Actual work in this frozen sequence | Count |
|---|---:|
| Events / controller invocations | 8 / 9 |
| Durable controller effects, all terminal after recovery | 68 |
| Native context reads / source expansions | 4 / 8 |
| Native input scans / additional accepted-candidate scans | 8 / 3 |
| Native symbolic attempts / produced candidate bodies | 4 / 3 |
| Native routing calls | 8 |
| Native world-memory service calls / compiled actions consumed | 11 / 2 |
| Native publication attempts / native reads / recovery calls | 7 / 4 / 1 |
| Controller behavioral scorer processes | 18 |
| Additional sealing admission/retrieval scorer processes | 24 |
| Total actual isolated scorer processes | 42 |
| Provider dispatches / provider tokens | 0 / 0 |

Total measured controller invocation wall time was **60.906438 seconds**. The
24 additional behavioral replays consumed **13.759197 seconds** within that
total. Context enrollment/priming, source inventory freeze, implementation,
and read-only audit time are excluded. Crash-time replays are counted from the
durable crash receipt; the recovered publish completion aliases the recovery
receipt and is not double-counted.

Both memory and ordinary controls perform a cheap current-source check before
repair. Both accepted no-op revisits without symbolic repair or a provider.
The world compiler's selected action dispatches that actual check. This
qualification shows no memory-specific reduction in checks. The unhandled
clamp task's zero tokens mean no provider was dispatched; its repair remains
incomplete and cannot be counted as successful token savings.

## What the seal establishes

The complete required set is frozen externally: accepted source, semantic
preparation, effect inventory, visible behavior, and audit behavior. Candidate
edits receive a new actual native scan before sealing, so the accepted source
and behavioral obligation bind the same bytes. Every seal uses genuine native
forest and aggregation bytes, native immutable storage, native integrity
verification, and exact independently rebuilt seal equality. All five receipt
leaves are replaced on each delta; no execution reuse is claimed.

The admission adapter also reads the complete terminal effect set from the
local durable ledger, checks immutable completion bytes and their source
bindings, and runs fresh isolated visible/audit checks. Stored parent chains
are recursively revalidated; each read also replays the requested current
source. Source for the next event comes from the native stored receipt, with
the local accepted file used as a checked cache.

This is the `integrity_only` native profile. Local execution receipts are
unsigned. Native verification checks bytes and digests, not a theorem about
Python execution. Admission relies on the trusted development controller,
scorer, local effect ledger, and a separate evaluation-owned CID metadata
index. It is not a production issuer policy or a self-contained portable
execution proof. The source profiles remain separate normal-import service
processes; they are not presented as a single deployed native runtime.

The qualified recovery boundary is a delta seal persisted before native CAS.
The controller does not blindly retry the same high-level publication ID. The
new adapter checks pending/current bindings and uses native CAS/WAL primitives
to finish it. Broader crash phases, concurrent publication, provider effects,
real repository edits, and reusable execution admission remain separate work.

## Evidence and preservation

`summary.json`, `audit_v3.json`, all `invocations/`, and `state/` retain actual
requests, subprocess outputs, completion receipts, ledger, native immutable
objects, pointers, WAL and crash receipt. The read-only audit passes **6,424
checks** and pins **339** sources actually loaded by controller workers.
`loaded_source_manifest.json` additionally includes the prior context
producer/consumer closure, for **483** native source files. `frozen_sources/`
contains **504** source/metadata files (20,354,387 bytes), including exact
controller, native service, runner and validator copies. `audit_archived.py`
passes the same checks using archived source hashes. A new executable replay
still needs a newly enrolled profile, reconstructed Python/assets and the
pinned scorer image; the image is not exported here.

The first run, `../development-v3-attempt1/`, is preserved. It stopped before
publication because the freeze omitted a native root `__init__.py` wrapper.
The second profile adds that real imported file. The initial read-only audit
also treated native pointer envelopes as payloads; `audit_attempt1.py` and
`audit_attempt1_failure.json` preserve that parser failure. No behavioral
outcomes were rerun to fix the audit.

